1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,774
It's June of nineteen fifty-six.

2
00:00:02,954 --> 00:00:04,510
School just let out.

3
00:00:04,690 --> 00:00:06,877
In a driveway on the South Side

4
00:00:06,877 --> 00:00:09,347
of Chicago, a father is packing the

5
00:00:09,347 --> 00:00:12,028
trunk of a two-tone Chevrolet Bel Air.

6
00:00:12,208 --> 00:00:13,746
Suitcases, a cooler,

7
00:00:13,746 --> 00:00:16,362
a water bag hanging from the front

8
00:00:16,362 --> 00:00:18,823
bumper, and a stack of road maps

9
00:00:18,823 --> 00:00:20,439
from the gas station.

10
00:00:20,619 --> 00:00:22,937
The kids are already fighting over the

11
00:00:22,937 --> 00:00:23,730
window seats.

12
00:00:23,910 --> 00:00:26,517
The family is going to California,

13
00:00:26,517 --> 00:00:27,974
on Route sixty-six.

14
00:00:28,323 --> 00:00:31,714
Route sixty-six ran almost two thousand four

15
00:00:31,714 --> 00:00:35,104
hundred miles, from Chicago to Santa Monica,

16
00:00:35,104 --> 00:00:35,951
California.

17
00:00:36,131 --> 00:00:38,431
Eight states, three time zones,

18
00:00:38,431 --> 00:00:40,953
and some of the most beautiful and

19
00:00:40,953 --> 00:00:42,807
empty country in America.

20
00:00:42,987 --> 00:00:45,541
John Steinbeck called it the Mother Road.

21
00:00:45,721 --> 00:00:47,009
And in the fifties,

22
00:00:47,009 --> 00:00:49,246
it was the most famous highway in

23
00:00:49,246 --> 00:00:49,924
the world.

24
00:00:50,274 --> 00:00:52,027
So let's take that trip.

25
00:00:52,207 --> 00:00:54,476
Let's ride along with a family in

26
00:00:54,476 --> 00:00:57,434
nineteen fifty-six, from the shores of Lake

27
00:00:57,434 --> 00:00:59,498
Michigan to the Pacific Ocean.

28
00:00:59,678 --> 00:01:01,502
The diners, the motels,

29
00:01:01,502 --> 00:01:04,833
the gas stations and the roadside wonders.

30
00:01:05,013 --> 00:01:06,560
If you ever took that road,

31
00:01:06,560 --> 00:01:08,394
I think you'll feel the hot wind

32
00:01:08,394 --> 00:01:09,482
through the window.

33
00:01:09,833 --> 00:01:13,614
Route sixty-six was created in nineteen twenty-six,

34
00:01:13,614 --> 00:01:16,357
as one of the first federal highways.

35
00:01:16,537 --> 00:01:18,571
At first, much of it was gravel

36
00:01:18,571 --> 00:01:19,161
and dirt.

37
00:01:19,341 --> 00:01:22,952
It wasn't fully paved until nineteen thirty-eight.

38
00:01:23,132 --> 00:01:26,030
During the Dust Bowl of the thirties,

39
00:01:26,030 --> 00:01:29,790
hundreds of thousands of desperate families from

40
00:01:29,790 --> 00:01:33,002
Oklahoma, Texas and Arkansas took it west

41
00:01:33,002 --> 00:01:35,508
to California, looking for work.

42
00:01:35,859 --> 00:01:38,023
After the Second World War,

43
00:01:38,023 --> 00:01:40,990
Route sixty-six became something new.

44
00:01:41,170 --> 00:01:43,078
Not a road of desperation,

45
00:01:43,078 --> 00:01:44,839
but a road of adventure.

46
00:01:45,019 --> 00:01:46,495
Americans had cars,

47
00:01:46,495 --> 00:01:49,448
money and paid vacations for the first

48
00:01:49,448 --> 00:01:49,837
time.

49
00:01:50,017 --> 00:01:50,968
And the West,

50
00:01:50,968 --> 00:01:52,212
with its deserts,

51
00:01:52,212 --> 00:01:53,969
canyons and movie stars,

52
00:01:53,969 --> 00:01:54,847
was calling.

53
00:01:55,027 --> 00:01:57,310
Families took to the road by the

54
00:01:57,310 --> 00:01:57,952
millions.

55
00:01:58,302 --> 00:01:59,969
In nineteen forty-six,

56
00:01:59,969 --> 00:02:02,848
a songwriter named Bobby Troup wrote a

57
00:02:02,848 --> 00:02:04,363
song about the trip.

58
00:02:04,543 --> 00:02:06,256
Nat King Cole recorded it,

59
00:02:06,256 --> 00:02:07,573
and it became a hit.

60
00:02:07,753 --> 00:02:10,423
Get your kicks on Route sixty-six.

61
00:02:10,603 --> 00:02:12,078
Everyone knew the words.

62
00:02:12,258 --> 00:02:14,552
The song named the towns along the

63
00:02:14,552 --> 00:02:16,712
way, and for millions of kids in

64
00:02:16,712 --> 00:02:17,657
the back seat,

65
00:02:17,657 --> 00:02:19,816
it became a map they could sing.

66
00:02:20,165 --> 00:02:22,395
Planning the trip was half the fun.

67
00:02:22,575 --> 00:02:25,146
Weeks before, Dad sent away for free

68
00:02:25,146 --> 00:02:26,789
maps and travel guides,

69
00:02:26,789 --> 00:02:29,218
or stopped at the auto club office

70
00:02:29,218 --> 00:02:30,218
for a TripTik,

71
00:02:30,218 --> 00:02:32,718
a little flip-book of maps with the

72
00:02:32,718 --> 00:02:34,719
route marked in highlighter.

73
00:02:34,899 --> 00:02:36,665
Mom packed sandwiches,

74
00:02:36,665 --> 00:02:38,993
a thermos and a coffee can of

75
00:02:38,993 --> 00:02:39,636
cookies.

76
00:02:39,816 --> 00:02:42,055
And the kids made lists of everything

77
00:02:42,055 --> 00:02:43,206
they wanted to see.

78
00:02:43,556 --> 00:02:45,568
The trip started in Chicago,

79
00:02:45,568 --> 00:02:46,934
near the lakefront.

80
00:02:47,114 --> 00:02:49,999
The first day took the family south

81
00:02:49,999 --> 00:02:52,719
across Illinois, past cornfields,

82
00:02:52,719 --> 00:02:55,357
small towns and grain elevators.

83
00:02:55,537 --> 00:02:57,708
They stopped at a diner for lunch,

84
00:02:57,708 --> 00:03:00,518
maybe a pork tenderloin sandwich bigger than

85
00:03:00,518 --> 00:03:01,029
the bun.

86
00:03:01,209 --> 00:03:04,043
And the kids started counting license plates

87
00:03:04,043 --> 00:03:05,202
from other states.

88
00:03:05,553 --> 00:03:08,450
By evening, they crossed the Mississippi River

89
00:03:08,450 --> 00:03:08,954
into St.

90
00:03:09,134 --> 00:03:11,615
Louis, on the Chain of Rocks Bridge,

91
00:03:11,615 --> 00:03:14,509
which had a strange twenty-two degree bend

92
00:03:14,509 --> 00:03:15,473
in the middle.

93
00:03:15,653 --> 00:03:17,686
Then on into the green hills of

94
00:03:17,686 --> 00:03:18,997
the Missouri Ozarks.

95
00:03:19,177 --> 00:03:20,351
Along the road,

96
00:03:20,351 --> 00:03:22,855
there were billboards for caves,

97
00:03:22,855 --> 00:03:25,437
souvenir shops and reptile farms,

98
00:03:25,437 --> 00:03:26,689
every few miles.

99
00:03:27,039 --> 00:03:30,762
Meramec Caverns in Missouri advertised on barn

100
00:03:30,762 --> 00:03:33,029
roofs all along the highway.

101
00:03:33,209 --> 00:03:34,861
The owner, Lester Dill,

102
00:03:34,861 --> 00:03:37,376
paid farmers to let him paint their

103
00:03:37,376 --> 00:03:37,807
barns.

104
00:03:37,987 --> 00:03:40,613
He also claimed that the outlaw Jesse

105
00:03:40,613 --> 00:03:43,026
James had once hidden in the cave.

106
00:03:43,206 --> 00:03:45,639
Families stopped to take the tour,

107
00:03:45,639 --> 00:03:48,288
and drove away with a bumper sticker,

108
00:03:48,288 --> 00:03:51,437
which Dill is often credited with inventing.

109
00:03:51,786 --> 00:03:53,807
Life in the back seat had its

110
00:03:53,807 --> 00:03:54,503
own rules.

111
00:03:54,683 --> 00:03:55,790
No seat belts,

112
00:03:55,790 --> 00:03:58,398
so kids sprawled across the seat,

113
00:03:58,398 --> 00:03:59,979
or lay on the floor,

114
00:03:59,979 --> 00:04:02,350
or on the shelf under the back

115
00:04:02,350 --> 00:04:02,903
window.

116
00:04:03,083 --> 00:04:04,383
They played I spy,

117
00:04:04,383 --> 00:04:07,199
twenty questions and the alphabet game,

118
00:04:07,199 --> 00:04:09,943
finding each letter on signs along the

119
00:04:09,943 --> 00:04:10,304
road.

120
00:04:10,484 --> 00:04:12,964
They read comic books until they got

121
00:04:12,964 --> 00:04:13,515
carsick.

122
00:04:13,695 --> 00:04:15,144
And every few miles,

123
00:04:15,144 --> 00:04:17,317
someone asked how much longer.

124
00:04:17,666 --> 00:04:20,201
At night, the family looked for a

125
00:04:20,201 --> 00:04:20,662
motel.

126
00:04:20,842 --> 00:04:22,619
In nineteen fifty-six,

127
00:04:22,619 --> 00:04:25,042
there were thousands of small,

128
00:04:25,042 --> 00:04:28,272
family-run motels along Route sixty-six.

129
00:04:28,452 --> 00:04:30,184
A neon sign out front,

130
00:04:30,184 --> 00:04:32,861
with a flashing arrow and the word

131
00:04:32,861 --> 00:04:33,491
Vacancy.

132
00:04:33,671 --> 00:04:36,032
Little cabins or rooms in a row,

133
00:04:36,032 --> 00:04:38,540
with a parking spot right in front

134
00:04:38,540 --> 00:04:39,499
of each door.

135
00:04:39,679 --> 00:04:41,984
A room might cost five or six

136
00:04:41,984 --> 00:04:43,255
dollars a night.

137
00:04:43,604 --> 00:04:46,178
Many motels had themes to catch a

138
00:04:46,178 --> 00:04:47,192
driver's eye.

139
00:04:47,372 --> 00:04:51,262
The Wigwam Motels in Arizona and California,

140
00:04:51,262 --> 00:04:54,709
where guests slept in concrete teepees.

141
00:04:54,889 --> 00:04:57,676
The Blue Swallow in Tucumcari,

142
00:04:57,676 --> 00:04:58,698
New Mexico.

143
00:04:58,878 --> 00:05:01,054
The Munger Moss in Lebanon,

144
00:05:01,054 --> 00:05:01,780
Missouri.

145
00:05:01,960 --> 00:05:04,724
A motel with a swimming pool was

146
00:05:04,724 --> 00:05:06,279
every kid's dream.

147
00:05:06,459 --> 00:05:08,220
After a day in a hot car,

148
00:05:08,220 --> 00:05:09,559
nothing was better.

149
00:05:09,908 --> 00:05:11,685
And motels were changing.

150
00:05:11,865 --> 00:05:13,501
In nineteen fifty-two,

151
00:05:13,501 --> 00:05:16,254
a man named Kemmons Wilson opened the

152
00:05:16,254 --> 00:05:18,411
first Holiday Inn in Memphis,

153
00:05:18,411 --> 00:05:21,907
Tennessee, after a frustrating family vacation.

154
00:05:22,087 --> 00:05:23,759
He promised clean rooms,

155
00:05:23,759 --> 00:05:26,476
a pool, air conditioning and free stays

156
00:05:26,476 --> 00:05:27,382
for children.

157
00:05:27,562 --> 00:05:28,846
Within a few years,

158
00:05:28,846 --> 00:05:31,551
Holiday Inns were popping up everywhere,

159
00:05:31,551 --> 00:05:34,390
and the little mom-and-pop motels began to

160
00:05:34,390 --> 00:05:35,607
feel the pressure.

161
00:05:35,957 --> 00:05:37,229
On the third day,

162
00:05:37,229 --> 00:05:39,696
the family crossed into Oklahoma.

163
00:05:39,876 --> 00:05:43,137
More of Route sixty-six ran through Oklahoma

164
00:05:43,137 --> 00:05:44,694
than any other state.

165
00:05:44,874 --> 00:05:48,072
They passed through Tulsa and Oklahoma City,

166
00:05:48,072 --> 00:05:51,051
and small towns with names like Chandler,

167
00:05:51,051 --> 00:05:52,432
Stroud and Clinton.

168
00:05:52,612 --> 00:05:54,041
The land got flatter,

169
00:05:54,041 --> 00:05:55,334
the sky got bigger,

170
00:05:55,334 --> 00:05:57,512
and the towns got farther apart.

171
00:05:57,861 --> 00:05:59,644
In the Texas Panhandle,

172
00:05:59,644 --> 00:06:02,125
the road ran straight as a ruler

173
00:06:02,125 --> 00:06:02,900
for miles.

174
00:06:03,080 --> 00:06:05,706
The town of Amarillo was famous for

175
00:06:05,706 --> 00:06:06,981
its steak houses.

176
00:06:07,161 --> 00:06:08,786
Not far from the highway,

177
00:06:08,786 --> 00:06:11,321
a restaurant called the Big Texan would

178
00:06:11,321 --> 00:06:14,116
later offer a free seventy-two ounce steak,

179
00:06:14,116 --> 00:06:15,741
if you could eat it in an

180
00:06:15,741 --> 00:06:16,066
hour.

181
00:06:16,246 --> 00:06:18,910
Kids stared at the cowboy hats and

182
00:06:18,910 --> 00:06:20,008
the oil wells.

183
00:06:20,357 --> 00:06:22,340
Halfway across Texas,

184
00:06:22,340 --> 00:06:25,550
at Adrian, a sign marked the exact

185
00:06:25,550 --> 00:06:28,194
midpoint of Route sixty-six.

186
00:06:28,374 --> 00:06:32,077
Eleven hundred and thirty-nine miles to Chicago,

187
00:06:32,077 --> 00:06:34,237
and the same to Los Angeles.

188
00:06:34,417 --> 00:06:36,965
Families stopped to take a picture with

189
00:06:36,965 --> 00:06:38,207
the Brownie camera,

190
00:06:38,207 --> 00:06:40,559
and Mom wrote a postcard to Grandma.

191
00:06:40,908 --> 00:06:42,162
And along the way,

192
00:06:42,162 --> 00:06:44,391
there were the diners and cafés.

193
00:06:44,571 --> 00:06:46,943
Places like the Midpoint Café,

194
00:06:46,943 --> 00:06:49,078
the U-Drop Inn in Shamrock,

195
00:06:49,078 --> 00:06:51,529
Texas, with its art deco tower,

196
00:06:51,529 --> 00:06:54,138
and countless little truck stops.

197
00:06:54,318 --> 00:06:57,111
Truckers knew where the best food was,

198
00:06:57,111 --> 00:06:59,756
so families looked for a parking lot

199
00:06:59,756 --> 00:07:01,005
full of big rigs.

200
00:07:01,185 --> 00:07:02,721
Chicken fried steak,

201
00:07:02,721 --> 00:07:04,179
biscuits and gravy,

202
00:07:04,179 --> 00:07:06,329
and a slice of homemade pie.

203
00:07:06,679 --> 00:07:08,361
Gas stops were frequent,

204
00:07:08,361 --> 00:07:10,816
because the big cars of the fifties

205
00:07:10,816 --> 00:07:13,270
got only about fifteen miles to the

206
00:07:13,270 --> 00:07:13,761
gallon.

207
00:07:13,941 --> 00:07:17,489
Gas cost around twenty-three cents a gallon

208
00:07:17,489 --> 00:07:19,304
in nineteen fifty-six.

209
00:07:19,484 --> 00:07:21,997
An attendant in a uniform and cap

210
00:07:21,997 --> 00:07:23,444
ran out to the car,

211
00:07:23,444 --> 00:07:24,587
pumped the gas,

212
00:07:24,587 --> 00:07:26,262
washed the windshield,

213
00:07:26,262 --> 00:07:28,547
checked the oil and the tires,

214
00:07:28,547 --> 00:07:30,755
and gave the kids a free road

215
00:07:30,755 --> 00:07:31,060
map.

216
00:07:31,410 --> 00:07:34,149
Every gas company competed for drivers.

217
00:07:34,329 --> 00:07:36,888
Phillips sixty-six, Texaco,

218
00:07:36,888 --> 00:07:40,774
Conoco, Sinclair with its green dinosaur,

219
00:07:40,774 --> 00:07:44,186
and Mobil with its flying red horse.

220
00:07:44,366 --> 00:07:46,352
Many gave away free gifts.

221
00:07:46,532 --> 00:07:48,820
Glasses, dishes, maps,

222
00:07:48,820 --> 00:07:51,524
and later, trading stamps.

223
00:07:51,704 --> 00:07:54,404
A clean restroom was a big selling

224
00:07:54,404 --> 00:07:58,693
point, and Texaco advertised its registered restrooms.

225
00:07:59,044 --> 00:08:00,247
In the desert,

226
00:08:00,247 --> 00:08:03,513
gas stations were few and far between.

227
00:08:03,693 --> 00:08:07,258
Signs warned, last gas for sixty miles.

228
00:08:07,438 --> 00:08:10,010
Cars overheated in the summer heat,

229
00:08:10,010 --> 00:08:12,582
so many drivers hung a canvas water

230
00:08:12,582 --> 00:08:14,346
bag on the front bumper.

231
00:08:14,526 --> 00:08:17,070
As the water seeped through the canvas

232
00:08:17,070 --> 00:08:19,145
and evaporated, it stayed cool,

233
00:08:19,145 --> 00:08:21,689
and could be poured into the radiator.

234
00:08:22,039 --> 00:08:24,697
And then there were the Burma-Shave signs.

235
00:08:24,877 --> 00:08:27,085
A series of small red signs,

236
00:08:27,085 --> 00:08:29,135
spaced along the roadside,

237
00:08:29,135 --> 00:08:31,184
each with a few words of a

238
00:08:31,184 --> 00:08:31,657
rhyme.

239
00:08:31,837 --> 00:08:33,666
You read them one by one as

240
00:08:33,666 --> 00:08:34,682
you drove past.

241
00:08:34,862 --> 00:08:36,130
Don't lose your head,

242
00:08:36,130 --> 00:08:37,157
to gain a minute,

243
00:08:37,157 --> 00:08:38,305
you need your head,

244
00:08:38,305 --> 00:08:39,634
your brains are in it.

245
00:08:39,814 --> 00:08:40,835
Burma-Shave.

246
00:08:41,015 --> 00:08:43,550
Kids waited eagerly for the next set,

247
00:08:43,550 --> 00:08:45,125
and read them out loud.

248
00:08:45,476 --> 00:08:46,520
In New Mexico,

249
00:08:46,520 --> 00:08:48,982
the landscape changed completely.

250
00:08:49,162 --> 00:08:51,211
Red mesas, dry riverbeds,

251
00:08:51,211 --> 00:08:54,572
and pueblos that had stood for centuries.

252
00:08:54,752 --> 00:08:57,488
Trading posts sold Navajo rugs,

253
00:08:57,488 --> 00:09:00,754
turquoise jewelry and Indian pottery.

254
00:09:00,934 --> 00:09:04,622
Albuquerque's Central Avenue glowed with neon at

255
00:09:04,622 --> 00:09:06,542
night, motel after motel.

256
00:09:06,722 --> 00:09:08,555
For a kid from Chicago,

257
00:09:08,555 --> 00:09:10,785
it felt like another planet.

258
00:09:11,136 --> 00:09:13,842
In Arizona, the family might take a

259
00:09:13,842 --> 00:09:16,163
side trip to the Grand Canyon.

260
00:09:16,343 --> 00:09:17,759
They stood at the rim,

261
00:09:17,759 --> 00:09:18,467
speechless.

262
00:09:18,647 --> 00:09:21,377
They drove through the Petrified Forest and

263
00:09:21,377 --> 00:09:22,583
the Painted Desert.

264
00:09:22,763 --> 00:09:23,951
And in Holbrook,

265
00:09:23,951 --> 00:09:26,105
they slept in a teepee at the

266
00:09:26,105 --> 00:09:29,447
Wigwam Motel, and bought rubber tomahawks and

267
00:09:29,447 --> 00:09:31,749
moccasins at the souvenir shop.

268
00:09:32,100 --> 00:09:34,241
Then came the scariest part of the

269
00:09:34,241 --> 00:09:34,619
drive.

270
00:09:34,799 --> 00:09:36,433
The Sitgreaves Pass,

271
00:09:36,433 --> 00:09:38,148
near Oatman, Arizona,

272
00:09:38,148 --> 00:09:41,743
a steep, twisting mountain road with hairpin

273
00:09:41,743 --> 00:09:43,704
turns and no guardrails.

274
00:09:43,884 --> 00:09:46,287
Some nervous drivers hired local men to

275
00:09:46,287 --> 00:09:48,382
drive their cars over the pass for

276
00:09:48,382 --> 00:09:48,690
them.

277
00:09:48,870 --> 00:09:51,737
Wild burros wandered the road in Oatman,

278
00:09:51,737 --> 00:09:53,456
and they still do today.

279
00:09:53,807 --> 00:09:55,599
Next came the Mojave Desert,

280
00:09:55,599 --> 00:09:57,968
the hottest and loneliest part of the

281
00:09:57,968 --> 00:09:58,288
trip.

282
00:09:58,468 --> 00:10:01,755
In summer, temperatures could reach a hundred

283
00:10:01,755 --> 00:10:03,216
and fifteen degrees.

284
00:10:03,396 --> 00:10:06,045
Most cars had no air conditioning,

285
00:10:06,045 --> 00:10:08,149
so families drove at night,

286
00:10:08,149 --> 00:10:09,863
with the windows down.

287
00:10:10,043 --> 00:10:12,574
Some hung wet towels in the windows.

288
00:10:12,754 --> 00:10:15,173
The kids slept in the back seat,

289
00:10:15,173 --> 00:10:16,760
sweaty and exhausted.

290
00:10:17,110 --> 00:10:19,412
At last, the road climbed over the

291
00:10:19,412 --> 00:10:21,918
Cajon Pass and dropped into the green

292
00:10:21,918 --> 00:10:24,017
valleys of Southern California.

293
00:10:24,197 --> 00:10:26,450
Orange groves stretched for miles.

294
00:10:26,630 --> 00:10:28,650
Palm trees lined the streets.

295
00:10:28,830 --> 00:10:31,528
The family passed through San Bernardino,

296
00:10:31,528 --> 00:10:32,976
where, as it happened,

297
00:10:32,976 --> 00:10:35,411
a pair of brothers named McDonald had

298
00:10:35,411 --> 00:10:37,978
a little hamburger stand that would one

299
00:10:37,978 --> 00:10:39,360
day change the world.

300
00:10:39,711 --> 00:10:42,509
And finally, after six or seven days,

301
00:10:42,509 --> 00:10:44,475
they reached Santa Monica,

302
00:10:44,475 --> 00:10:46,441
and the end of the road at

303
00:10:46,441 --> 00:10:47,803
the Pacific Ocean.

304
00:10:47,983 --> 00:10:50,567
The kids ran into the cold water,

305
00:10:50,567 --> 00:10:51,350
screaming.

306
00:10:51,530 --> 00:10:53,450
Dad stood on the beach and looked

307
00:10:53,450 --> 00:10:54,439
out at the ocean,

308
00:10:54,439 --> 00:10:56,533
a little amazed that they'd made it.

309
00:10:56,713 --> 00:10:58,756
Maybe they went to Disneyland,

310
00:10:58,756 --> 00:11:01,138
which had opened the summer before,

311
00:11:01,138 --> 00:11:02,704
in nineteen fifty-five.

312
00:11:03,055 --> 00:11:05,294
The trip home was just as long,

313
00:11:05,294 --> 00:11:07,101
and the family was tired,

314
00:11:07,101 --> 00:11:08,546
sunburned and happy.

315
00:11:08,726 --> 00:11:11,910
They had a shoebox full of postcards,

316
00:11:11,910 --> 00:11:13,459
a rubber tomahawk,

317
00:11:13,459 --> 00:11:15,697
a jar of desert sand and a

318
00:11:15,697 --> 00:11:17,074
hundred stories.

319
00:11:17,254 --> 00:11:19,131
Back at school in September,

320
00:11:19,131 --> 00:11:21,812
the kids told their classmates about the

321
00:11:21,812 --> 00:11:24,092
Grand Canyon and the teepee motel.

322
00:11:24,272 --> 00:11:26,315
It was the summer they'd never forget.

323
00:11:26,666 --> 00:11:28,407
But the end was already coming.

324
00:11:28,587 --> 00:11:30,071
In nineteen fifty-six,

325
00:11:30,071 --> 00:11:31,826
the same year as our trip,

326
00:11:31,826 --> 00:11:35,064
President Eisenhower signed the act creating the

327
00:11:35,064 --> 00:11:36,819
interstate highway system.

328
00:11:36,999 --> 00:11:39,261
Over the next twenty-five years,

329
00:11:39,261 --> 00:11:42,939
five different interstates replaced Route sixty-six,

330
00:11:42,939 --> 00:11:43,999
piece by piece.

331
00:11:44,179 --> 00:11:46,896
The new highways were faster and safer.

332
00:11:47,076 --> 00:11:49,085
And they went around the little towns.

333
00:11:49,435 --> 00:11:52,217
When the interstate bypassed a town,

334
00:11:52,217 --> 00:11:54,613
the traffic simply disappeared.

335
00:11:54,793 --> 00:11:57,900
Diners, motels and gas stations that had

336
00:11:57,900 --> 00:12:00,775
been busy for thirty years went quiet

337
00:12:00,775 --> 00:12:02,095
almost overnight.

338
00:12:02,275 --> 00:12:03,332
Many closed.

339
00:12:03,512 --> 00:12:05,578
Some towns nearly died.

340
00:12:05,758 --> 00:12:07,706
In nineteen eighty-four,

341
00:12:07,706 --> 00:12:10,708
the last piece of Route sixty-six was

342
00:12:10,708 --> 00:12:12,493
bypassed, at Williams,

343
00:12:12,493 --> 00:12:13,142
Arizona.

344
00:12:13,322 --> 00:12:15,007
In nineteen eighty-five,

345
00:12:15,007 --> 00:12:17,815
the road was officially removed from the

346
00:12:17,815 --> 00:12:19,429
federal highway system.

347
00:12:19,780 --> 00:12:21,428
But people didn't forget it.

348
00:12:21,608 --> 00:12:22,806
In the nineties,

349
00:12:22,806 --> 00:12:25,425
fans began working to save what was

350
00:12:25,425 --> 00:12:25,799
left.

351
00:12:25,979 --> 00:12:27,976
Old motels were restored.

352
00:12:28,156 --> 00:12:29,654
Neon signs were relit.

353
00:12:29,834 --> 00:12:32,806
Historic Route sixty-six signs went up.

354
00:12:32,986 --> 00:12:36,383
Today, travelers from all over the world,

355
00:12:36,383 --> 00:12:39,117
especially from Europe and Japan,

356
00:12:39,117 --> 00:12:41,354
come to drive the old road,

357
00:12:41,354 --> 00:12:45,165
looking for the America of nineteen fifty-six.

358
00:12:45,515 --> 00:12:48,359
Route sixty-six was more than a highway.

359
00:12:48,539 --> 00:12:50,838
It was the great American adventure.

360
00:12:51,018 --> 00:12:52,112
The open road,

361
00:12:52,112 --> 00:12:53,128
the wide sky,

362
00:12:53,128 --> 00:12:55,395
a family together in the car,

363
00:12:55,395 --> 00:12:58,053
and a new place around every bend.

364
00:12:58,233 --> 00:13:00,222
For a generation of families,

365
00:13:00,222 --> 00:13:02,553
that trip west was the vacation of

366
00:13:02,553 --> 00:13:03,307
a lifetime.

367
00:13:03,657 --> 00:13:05,612
If you ever made that trip as

368
00:13:05,612 --> 00:13:07,163
a kid, you remember it.

369
00:13:07,343 --> 00:13:07,865
The heat.

370
00:13:08,045 --> 00:13:08,510
The neon.

371
00:13:08,690 --> 00:13:09,978
The teepee motel.

372
00:13:10,158 --> 00:13:11,679
The pie at the diner.

373
00:13:11,859 --> 00:13:13,937
The first sight of the Pacific.

374
00:13:14,117 --> 00:13:15,221
And your father,

375
00:13:15,221 --> 00:13:16,876
both hands on the wheel,

376
00:13:16,876 --> 00:13:18,738
singing along to the radio.

377
00:13:18,918 --> 00:13:21,414
Get your kicks on Route sixty-six.

378
00:13:21,764 --> 00:13:23,657
So tell us in the comments.

379
00:13:23,837 --> 00:13:26,484
Did you ever travel on Route sixty-six?

380
00:13:26,664 --> 00:13:27,743
Where did you go,

381
00:13:27,743 --> 00:13:29,648
and what do you remember most?

382
00:13:29,828 --> 00:13:31,894
And what was the best family road

383
00:13:31,894 --> 00:13:33,334
trip of your childhood?

384
00:13:33,514 --> 00:13:34,814
We read every comment.

385
00:13:34,994 --> 00:13:37,432
Next time, we're looking at what it

386
00:13:37,432 --> 00:13:40,219
meant to turn sixteen in nineteen sixty.

387
00:13:40,399 --> 00:13:42,106
Thank you for spending this time with

388
00:13:42,106 --> 00:13:42,245
me.
